<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman Senate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman Senate was the governing council of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, composed largely of former magistrates and members of leading families. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> it directed foreign policy, finance and war, while under the emperors its authority became largely formal. It appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> profile as a central institution of Roman government and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> profile as the body that directed Rome's wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-senate.jpg" alt="Roman Senate" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cesare Maccari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cicero_Denounces_Catiline_in_the_Roman_Senate_by_Cesare_Maccari.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word senatus comes from senex, 'old man', and means an assembly of elders. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a>, traditionally founded in 753 BCE, the heads of the leading clans formed a board of elders. The senate is said to have been created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s first king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. It served as the king's council, and its most important function was to choose new kings: the interrex nominated a candidate, the senate approved him, the people elected him and the senate approved him again. After the last king was overthrown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> and Publius Valerius Publicola are said to have raised the senate to 300 members in 509 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In the Republic the senate had 300 to 500 members who served for life, at first only patricians. Its decrees, the senatus consulta, were formally advice to a magistrate and had no legal force, but they were usually obeyed. Only the senate could authorise spending from the treasury, and it decided which magistrate governed which province. After 202 BCE the senatus consultum ultimum replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in emergencies.</p><p>Senators wore a toga with a broad purple stripe and an iron, later gold, ring. They could not engage in banking or public contracts, could not leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> without permission and were paid no salary. Meetings opened with a sacrifice and a search for omens and had to end by nightfall, so a determined senator could talk a proposal to death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The senate reached the apex of its republican power in the middle Republic and declined in the late Republic after the reforms of the tribunes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principate">Principate</a> it kept its legal position but little real authority, though the assemblies' powers passed to it. Around 300 CE Diocletian's reforms ended any illusion that it had independent powers, and Constantine created a second senate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>The Western senate worked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> and the Ostrogothic kings and was restored after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>'s reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, but the long war had mortally weakened it, and it had disappeared by the early 7th century. The Eastern senate lasted into the early 13th century; its last known act was to elect Nicholas Kanabos as emperor in 1204.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman Senate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome's victory over Antiochus III and his Aetolian allies in 191 BCE ended Aetolian control: the consul Manius Acilius Glabrio restored the sanctuary's property and the autonomy of the Delphians, which the <b>Roman Senate</b> soon confirmed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Administration below the king relied on local chiefs and town councils of Punic type with officials called sufetes, while the <b>Roman Senate</b> increasingly acted as arbiter of disputed successions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After destroying Carthage, the <b>Roman Senate</b> ordered the farming treatise of the Carthaginian writer Mago, in twenty-eight books, to be translated into Latin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early Greek cities and in Rome aristocrats governed through a council of elders or former magistrates, such as the Areopagus at Athens or the <b>Roman Senate</b>, and through annual offices reserved for men of their own families.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>The authors of The Federalist signed themselves Publius, after a founder of the Roman Republic, and the United States, like many other countries, gave its upper house the name of the <b>Roman Senate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Only a mixed constitution could slow the decay, and Polybius found one in the Roman Republic, where the consuls, the <b>Senate</b> and the assemblies stood for monarchy, aristocracy and democracy and checked one another.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li></ul></section>
